WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Machine learning fundamentals in fifteen minutes. Let's build the mental model everything else sits on.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:12.800
Supervised learning means learning from labeled examples. You have inputs X and targets y.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.900
Classification predicts a category like spam or not spam. Regression predicts a continuous number like price.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:27.100
The core workflow: split your data into train, validation, and test sets. Never leak test data into training.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:34.400
The model learns parameters by minimizing a loss function through gradient descent.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:41.700
Overfitting is when the model memorizes training data and fails on new data. Watch the validation score.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:48.900
Regularization like L2 weight decay, dropout, and early stopping all fight overfitting.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:56.100
Cross-validation rotates which fold is the validation set, giving you a more reliable performance estimate.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:03.600
Bias variance trade-off: simple models underfit with high bias, complex models overfit with high variance.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.800
Feature engineering still matters. Garbage in, garbage out applies even to deep learning.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:18.100
Evaluation metrics must match your problem. Accuracy lies on imbalanced data; use precision, recall, and F1.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:25.400
For ranking problems, mean average precision and NDCG are the standard choices.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:32.700
Start simple: logistic regression and gradient boosted trees are strong baselines before neural networks.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:40.000
If a simple baseline matches your complex model, ship the simple model. Complexity is a cost.
